 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 6,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_rock",
   "title": "The Rock",
   "text": "The Rock of Gibraltar is a ridge of Jurassic limestone 426 metres high, about 5 kilometres long and some 1.5 kilometres wide. It was one of the two Pillars of Hercules in the old stories, with Jebel Musa opposite in Africa; the ancient Greeks and Romans thought it the edge of the world. To the Moors it was Jabal Tariq, the Mountain of Tariq, which turned into 'Gibraltar'."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_macaques",
   "title": "The Barbary Macaques",
   "text": "Gibraltar's macaques are the only wild monkeys in Europe, and are tailless, which is why they are also called Barbary apes. There are around three hundred of them in several troops. The old saying goes that when the apes leave the Rock, the British will leave too, and in the Second World War Winston Churchill is said to have asked for their numbers to be restored. They are still here."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_stmichaels",
   "title": "St Michael's Cave",
   "text": "A natural cavern of stalactites and stalagmites in the limestone, with chambers that run deep into the Rock. In ancient times people thought it was bottomless and that it joined to Africa by a secret tunnel. In the Second World War it was fitted out as a hospital that was never needed. Today it hosts concerts, because the cave has remarkable acoustics."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_tunnels",
   "title": "The Great Siege Tunnels",
   "text": "During the Great Siege of 1779 to 1783, Spanish and French forces tried to take Gibraltar by land and sea. The British garrison needed to fire cannons down on the besiegers from the north face, so they dug a tunnel in the rock with hand tools and gunpowder. The tunnels have windows in the cliff for the guns, and more were added in the Second World War, with over 50 kilometres in all."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_moors",
   "title": "The Moorish Castle",
   "text": "The Moors landed in 711 under Tariq ibn Ziyad and took the Rock, and the castle's Tower of Homage dates from 1333, when it was rebuilt. The tower is the only large part of the Moorish fortress still standing, though the walls have lived through a great deal of cannon fire. Tariq's name is part of the word Gibraltar."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 48,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_europa",
   "title": "Europa Point",
   "text": "Europa Point is the southernmost tip of Gibraltar, with a lighthouse built in 1841 that is white with a red band. On a clear day the coast of Morocco can be seen across the Strait, 14 kilometres away. Ships from half the world pass by, going in and out of the Mediterranean."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 48,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_mosque",
   "title": "The Ibrahim-al-Ibrahim Mosque",
   "text": "The mosque at Europa Point was a gift from King Fahd of Saudi Arabia and opened in 1997. It is the largest mosque in Gibraltar and one of several places of worship, with synagogues and churches too, that make the Rock unusually mixed for a place so small."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_levanter",
   "title": "The Levanter",
   "text": "The Levanter is the east wind that funnels through the Strait, and it brings a cloud that sits on the top of the Rock like a tablecloth. The air is forced up the east face and cools into cloud, which tumbles down the western side but melts before it hits the town. It is harmless, and it makes the Rock look like it is wearing a hat."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "gibraltar_migration",
   "title": "Migration at the Strait",
   "text": "Each autumn, thousands of raptors, including black kites, honey buzzards and eagles, fly over the Strait to cross to Africa, because it is the shortest sea crossing and they like to stay over land as long as possible. Gibraltar is one of the best places in Europe to watch it, and many birdwatchers come for the sight."
  }
 ],
